---
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import BaseHead from "../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import TableOfContents from "../../components/TableOfContents.astro";
import DocSection from "../../components/docs/DocSection.astro";
import DocAlert from "../../components/docs/DocAlert.astro";
import DocNavigation from "../../components/docs/DocNavigation.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
import { wasmSectionPages } from "../../data/wasm-pages";

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "overview", text: "Overview" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "copy-cost", text: "Copy cost & encoding" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "batching", text: "Batching & op streams" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "async", text: "Async vs synchronous host calls" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "ffi-hygiene", text: "FFI hygiene" },
];

const navPages = wasmSectionPages.map((p) => ({
  title: p.titleEn,
  href: `/wasm/${p.slug}`,
  description: p.descEn,
}));
const currentPath = "/wasm/js-wasm-boundary";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead
      title="JS ↔ WASM boundary – contenteditable.lab"
      description="Strings across wasm-bindgen, avoiding per-keystroke full document copies, and async timing with browser input events."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <SiteNav />
    <main class="flex max-w-full m-0 p-0">
      <section
        class="flex-1 flex max-w-[1120px] mx-auto py-7 px-5 pb-10 gap-6 w-full"
      >
        <aside class="hidden lg:block w-[220px] shrink-0">
          <TableOfContents headings={headings} />
        </aside>
        <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
          <header class="mb-8">
            <nav class="mb-4 text-sm">
              <a href="/" class="text-text-muted no-underline">Home</a>
              <span class="text-text-faint mx-2">/</span>
              <a href="/wasm" class="text-text-muted no-underline">Wasm</a>
              <span class="text-text-faint mx-2">/</span>
              <span class="text-text-primary">JS ↔ WASM boundary</span>
            </nav>
            <h1 class="text-3xl leading-tight m-0 mb-2">JS ↔ WASM boundary</h1>
            <p class="m-0 text-[0.95rem] text-text-secondary">
              wasm-bindgen makes calls easy; the expensive part is often data
              movement and allocation—not Rust’s inner loops.
            </p>
          </header>

          <article
            class="rounded-xl border border-border-light bg-bg-surface p-6 px-7 text-[0.9rem] leading-[1.7]"
          >
            <DocSection id="overview" title="Overview">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Browser events arrive on the JS side. Each crossing into WASM may
                copy linear-memory buffers or convert strings. A naive “serialize
                whole document to Rust on every <code>input</code>” design will
                bottleneck before your diff algorithm matters.
              </p>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="copy-cost" title="Copy cost & encoding">
              <ul class="m-0 pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                  <strong>UTF-16 vs UTF-8:</strong> JS exposes strings as UTF-16
                  code units; Rust <code>String</code> indices differ. Pass{" "}
                  <strong>byte offsets</strong> or <strong>scalar offsets</strong>{" "}
                  explicitly across the boundary, or normalize in one place.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Large strings:</strong> Full HTML serialization from JS
                  to Rust duplicates memory. Prefer <strong>incremental patches</strong>,{" "}
                  <strong>hashed snapshots</strong>, or shared views where your
                  security model allows.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="batching" title="Batching & op streams">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Send <strong>small operations</strong> (insert/delete/format) when
                possible instead of shipping the whole doc. For collaboration,
                binary CRDT updates are often smaller than text snapshots.
              </p>
              <DocAlert type="info">
                <p class="m-0 text-sm">
                  Debouncing helps for expensive validation—not for losing IME
                  ordering; never batch in ways that reorder composition relative
                  to commits.
                </p>
              </DocAlert>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="async" title="Async vs synchronous host calls">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Promises from WASM resolve on microtasks; input handlers may expect
                synchronous DOM updates. If Rust work is async, guard against
                selection races: the user may type again before your WASM task
                finishes.
              </p>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="ffi-hygiene" title="FFI hygiene">
              <ul class="m-0 pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Prefer stable handles for long-lived documents (e.g. boxed Rust state in WASM linear memory).</li>
                <li>Expose narrow APIs: “apply op” vs “replace entire tree”.</li>
                <li>Measure with browser Performance panel: time in WASM vs glue.</li>
              </ul>
            </DocSection>

            <DocNavigation
              current={currentPath}
              pages={navPages}
              title="Wasm guides"
            />
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
